# Keep specs in sync with feature changes

When you change **user-visible or behavioral** firmware/backend behavior (wake/sleep, pulses, upload, LEDs, storage, stay-awake, API payload/contract), do **not** leave the docs stale.

## Required action

After (or as part of) the feature work, **spawn a dedicated subagent** (`Task` tool) whose only job is to update the normative docs to match the new code.

Point the subagent at the right SoT files:

| Change area | Update |
| --- | --- |
| Product requirements / goals / constraints | [docs/intent_spec.md](docs/intent_spec.md) |
| Firmware behavior, wakes, concurrency, hardware assumptions | [docs/firmware/fw_specification.md](docs/firmware/fw_specification.md) |
| Upload JSON / HTTP contract | [docs/api/upload.md](docs/api/upload.md) |

## Subagent brief (use this shape)

```text
Feature change summary: <what changed>
Code touched: <paths>
Update normative docs to match code. Prefer intent_spec for requirements-only
deltas; fw_specification for how firmware behaves now; api/upload.md for
wire contract. Do not invent behavior not in code. Cite what you changed.
Ignore docs/archive/ except as historical reference.
```

## Do / don't

- **Do** spawn the subagent even for small behavior tweaks (LED meaning, wake priority, upload error paths).
- **Do** keep intent_spec free of pin maps / library choices; put those in fw_specification.
- **Don't** treat `docs/archive/` as living truth.
- **Don't** skip the subagent because the parent agent “already knows” the docs — use a separate pass so the SoT stays aligned.

Pure refactors with **no** behavior or contract change do not need a spec update.
